  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Ubigi</strong> if you'd rather buy from an established telecom brand and have a real discount code to apply at checkout. Pick <strong>BNEsim</strong> if you want the cheapest per-GB price and don't mind that the discount is already baked into the sticker price instead of something you type in yourself. They're not really competing on the same thing: one sells pedigree and a working code, the other sells a lower price with no code needed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: MVNO Pedigree With a Code, or Pre-Discounted Budget Pricing</h2>
  <p>
    Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler that's been running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and is now part of NTT Group, one of the largest telecom companies in the world. Transatel also supplies the SIM connectivity inside connected cars from BMW, Toyota, Jeep, Maserati, Fiat, Alfa Romeo, Mini, Jaguar, and Land Rover, so the network your phone's eSIM rides on is the same infrastructure running in someone's dashboard. That pedigree shows up in the price: a 1GB US plan runs about $4 before any code, and {{ providerA.primaryCode }} (our own code) takes another 10% off.
  </p>
  <p>
    BNEsim doesn't have that kind of corporate backing, and it doesn't advertise a discount code anywhere on its own site either. What it does instead is apply the discount directly to the sticker price: its pricing pages show up to 70% off as a struck-through original price next to a lower sale price, no code required. On a straight per-GB basis that makes BNEsim meaningfully cheaper than Ubigi in most destinations we've checked.
  </p>

